"A query execution plan displays the database operators selected to execute a query and their estimated or observed behavior.\n\nUse when: Investigate expensive joins, scans, and row-estimate errors. Compare plans for a query before and after a justified change.\n\nAvoid when: The aim is a general database schema or application call graph.\n\nVisual grammar: Operator nodes encode scans, joins, sorts, and aggregations. Tree edges connect producer operators to consuming operators. Metrics explicitly distinguish estimates from actual rows, time, loops, and engine-specific cost.\n\nBuild: Capture the query, plan format, engine version, and relevant execution context. Lay out operators with a declared dataflow direction. Show the metrics needed for the investigation, preserving units. Place predicates in compact side notes rather than across operator links.\n\nCheck: A chosen edge direction is consistent throughout the tree. Estimated cost is not reported as milliseconds. Repeated loops and total versus per-loop values are interpreted correctly. Predicates and metrics remain legible without intersecting edges.\n\nPitfalls: Treating an estimated plan as observed execution evidence. Comparing plans collected under materially different conditions without disclosure.\n\nExample: A join plan shows an index scan feeding a nested loop and a final aggregate, with estimates separated from measured rows.\n\nAlternatives: B-tree index diagram, Relational schema diagram."
